  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place through the upcoming week,\nbringing continued dry weather conditions and near average\ntemperatures. High pressure will drift to our southeast today,\nallowing for light southeasterly return flow. This onshore flow\nwill limit high temperatures across coastal locations to the 50s\nwhile inland areas warm into the lower 60s. Other than some\npassing high altitude cirrus, it will be a mainly sunny dry day.\nTonight will be cool, although not as cold as previous nights\nwith lows falling into the middle to upper 30s in most\nlocations. \n\nA sfc cold front will cross on Tuesday but will bring little fan\nfare as the dry airmass will prevent any beneficial rainfall.\nHigh temperatures will be mainly into the lower 60s with mostly\nsunny skies prevailing. Tuesday night will feature low\ntemperatures at or below freezing in most locations. Canadian\nhigh pressure will build to our north on Wednesday, with mostly\nsunny skies and highs into the 50s. A stronger cold front will\nthen cross Thursday into Thursday night as a nor'easter moves\nwell offshore to our southeast. A trof will then move in behind\nthis front for Friday with cooler temperatures to follow. Dry\nweather and near avg temperatures look likely for the upcoming\nweekend.",
